<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 12+ (API 31+) backup/transfer rules. Referenced from AndroidManifest as
  android:dataExtractionRules.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS EVEN THOUGH allowBackup="false":

  android:allowBackup="false" disables CLOUD backup (Auto Backup / Google Drive, and adb backup).
  It does NOT disable device-to-device transfer on Android 12+. D2D transfer is governed solely by
  the <device-transfer> section below; an app with allowBackup="false" and no dataExtractionRules
  still has its private data copied wholesale onto a new handset during "copy apps & data".

  For STIQ that is not a cosmetic gap, it is two concrete failures:

    1. PRIVACY. app_ArtiState/ holds Tor guard state — the small, stable set of entry relays this
       install has committed to. Guard identities are a long-lived pseudonymous fingerprint of the
       user. Off-device, they are a linkable identifier; restored onto a second device, they link
       the two installs together. The SQLCipher event cache (stiq-cache-<cid>__<slot>.db) holds
       decrypted-at-rest community content, DMs and the phonebook. Neither belongs in any transfer.

    2. CORRECTNESS. Guard state is meaningful only for the device and network that built it.
       Restoring it onto different hardware on a different network is precisely the situation
       Tor's guard algorithm is designed to avoid: it either forces reconnection to guards chosen
       under someone else's network conditions, or trips arti's state-directory locking. The
       Keystore-wrapped keys that the cache and the key vault are encrypted under are hardware-bound
       and CANNOT be transferred, so a restored database is unreadable ciphertext anyway — a
       "successful" transfer would hand the user a broken install.

  So both channels deny everything. All NINE domains the platform accepts are listed explicitly
  rather than relying on a bare <exclude domain="root" /> to sweep up its subdirectories, because
  "root" vs "file"/"database"/"sharedpref" containment is not worth being subtle about in a rule
  whose failure mode is silent data exfiltration.

  DO NOT "TIDY UP" THE device_* ENTRIES — THEY ARE NOT DUPLICATES OF THE UNPREFIXED ONES.
  The two groups name two physically disjoint directory trees, so neither group covers the other
  and deleting either one opens a real hole:

    root / file / database / sharedpref  -> CREDENTIAL-protected storage, /data/user/<u>/<pkg>/
                                            (Context.createCredentialProtectedStorageContext())
    device_root / device_file /          -> DEVICE-protected storage, /data/user_de/<u>/<pkg>/
    device_database / device_sharedpref     (Context.createDeviceProtectedStorageContext())

  Both sets are part of the documented schema and are parsed, not ignored: see AOSP
  frameworks/base/core/java/android/app/backup/FullBackup.java, BackupScheme#getTokenForXmlDomain
  and #getDirectoryForCriteriaDomain, which accept exactly these nine strings and map the device_*
  four onto the DE-storage dirs assigned from createDeviceProtectedStorageContext(). Any OTHER
  string is what gets silently dropped ("domain=... invalid; skipping", verbose log only), so do
  not invent domain names here either.

  ("external" is the ninth; it resolves to getExternalFilesDir(), which is null when no external
  volume is mounted, so it is listed for completeness and costs nothing when absent.)

  Note the deliberate absence of any <include>: in these rules an <exclude> is meaningless without
  the surrounding section, and a section containing only excludes means "back up nothing else that
  would otherwise be eligible" — which, combined with allowBackup="false", is total denial.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <!-- Cloud backup. Already dead via allowBackup="false"; stated here so flipping that attribute
         back on can never silently start uploading the cache or the Tor state directory. -->
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
        <exclude domain="device_file" />
        <exclude domain="device_database" />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" />
    </cloud-backup>

    <!-- Device-to-device transfer. THIS is the channel allowBackup="false" does not close.
         Excluded in full: app_ArtiState/ (Tor guard state), databases/ + files/ (the SQLCipher
         event cache), and shared_prefs/ (stiq_push endpoint, stiq_fgs state). -->
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
        <exclude domain="device_file" />
        <exclude domain="device_database" />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
